═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
FIELD INTERPRETATION GUIDE
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
- "building": from sheet name pattern ("BUILDING A"), or a "Subject:" header
  cell. If unknown, use the sheet name verbatim, confidence "low".
- "trade": from a "Scope:" header cell, or inferred from file/sheet name. If
  inferred rather than explicitly stated, confidence "medium" or lower.
- "weightPct": if the column sums to ~1.0 instead of ~100, multiply by 100 —
  always normalize to 0-100.
- "accToDatePct": only extract from a clear "% Complete" / "To-Date %" /
  "Accomplishment %" column. Never guess from narrative text.
- "level"/floor: only fill if explicitly named (Ground Floor, 2nd Floor,
  Podium, Roof Deck, Basement, Mezzanine, Penthouse). Leave null otherwise.
- Section-header rows (no dates, no amounts) inform category/subcategory for
  rows beneath them — they do not become their own records.
- Dates wildly outside a reasonable construction timeframe (e.g. year 1900 or
  2099) usually mean an uncalculated spreadsheet formula — still output the
  record with confidence "low" and note it in dataIssues, don't silently fix.
- "violations": look for NCR logs, safety inspection reports, QA/QC checklist
  flags, non-compliance notices. "severity" should only be filled if the
  source explicitly grades it (Critical/Major/Minor or equivalent wording) —
  otherwise "unspecified".
- "quantityTakedowns": look for Bill of Quantities (BOQ), material takeoff
  sheets, or drawing-quantity schedules — distinct from materialEntries
  (which track stock/consumption), takedowns track measured quantities of
  work/material required per scope.
- "forecastNotes": look for narrative sections in progress reports, PM
  reports, minutes of meeting, or risk registers that discuss what MIGHT
  happen (delays, overruns, shortages) rather than what already happened.
  Do not convert a forecast into a task or cost entry — keep it as a
  narrative risk record. Only fill "quantifiedImpact" if a specific number
  or day-count is explicitly stated in the source; otherwise leave "".
- Common contractor report types to recognize and route correctly:
  · Weekly/Monthly Progress Report → tasks (accToDatePct) + forecastNotes
  · S-Curve / Physical Progress Report → tasks (weightPct + accToDatePct)
  · Manpower Report → note in meta.blindSpots if no dedicated array fits;
    do not force into materialEntries or costEntries
  · Delay Notice / Extension of Time (EOT) request → forecastNotes
    (topic: schedule_delay) + relevant tasks if specific activities named
  · Variation Order (VO) → costEntries (type: budget, note field describing
    the VO) — flag in dataIssues that this is a variation, not base budget
  · RFI (Request for Information) → meta.skippedSections unless it directly
    changes a quantity, cost, or date already reflected elsewhere
  · Minutes of Meeting → forecastNotes for any risk/delay discussion +
    violations for any non-compliance items raised
  · NCR / Non-Conformance Report → violations (type: quality_ncr)

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
WHAT NOT TO DO
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
- Do not average, total, or calculate anything not already in the source.
- Do not fabricate a p6ActivityId, resourceCode, or loggedBy if none exists —
  use "" or null.
- Do not translate, rename, or "clean up" trade/building names — output them
  exactly as written, even if inconsistent across rows.
- Do not omit a row just because it looks incomplete — output it with nulls
  and a dataIssues note instead of dropping it silently.
- Do not resolve a violation's status yourself (e.g. assuming "closed" just
  because a corrective action is mentioned) — only mark it from an explicit
  status field or clear closing statement in the source.
- Do not convert a forecast/projection into a confirmed task or cost entry —
  a risk statement about a possible future delay is not the same as a
  scheduled activity.

